HYBRID-POWERED GROUND DRILLING VEHICLE FOR AGRICULTURAL PURPOSE

1. Project Description

In earlier days, soil digging for plantation was carried out manually by humans using traditional tools such as hand augers. This process required considerable physical effort, consumed more time, and involved more human labour, especially during large-scale plantation activities.

To overcome these difficulties, this project proposes a Hybrid-Powered Ground Drilling Vehicle for Agricultural Purpose. The vehicle operates using two separate power systems: an engine-powered drilling system and a battery-powered electric drive system.

The engine is used to operate the auger drilling mechanism and provide vertical movement of the drilling assembly. A rechargeable battery supplies electrical power to DC motors, which are responsible for the forward and backward movement of the vehicle.

The vehicle consists of a supporting frame, wheels, an engine-powered drilling arrangement, a battery-powered drive system, and an operator seating arrangement. The main aim is to reduce manual labour, save time, improve drilling efficiency, and make agricultural plantation easier.

2. Objectives

- To reduce manual soil digging operations.
- To minimize human physical effort during plantation.
- To save time during soil drilling activities.
- To develop an engine-powered auger drilling mechanism.
- To provide vertical movement of the drilling assembly.
- To develop a battery-powered vehicle drive system using DC motors.
- To improve the efficiency of agricultural plantation activities.

3. Working Principle

The vehicle works using two independent power systems.

3.1 Engine-Powered Drilling System

The engine provides mechanical power to the auger drilling mechanism. The auger drill bit rotates and penetrates the soil to create holes for planting saplings. The engine-powered arrangement also operates the vertical movement mechanism, allowing the drilling assembly to move downward and upward during operation.

3.2 Battery-Powered Vehicle Drive System

A rechargeable battery supplies electrical energy to the DC motors. These motors drive the vehicle wheels and provide forward and backward movement. This allows the operator to position the vehicle at different plantation locations.

3.3 Overall Working Procedure

1. The vehicle is moved to the required location using the battery-powered DC motor drive system.
2. The vehicle is positioned at the selected drilling point.
3. The engine is operated to drive the auger drilling mechanism.
4. The drilling assembly moves vertically downward to penetrate the soil.
5. After completing the drilling operation, the assembly moves upward.
6. The vehicle is moved to the next location for the subsequent drilling operation.

